/* ---------- 2.2.6 Defences ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.2.6', unit: 'TO', ref: '2.2.6', title: 'Defences in tort',
  short: 'Volenti non fit injuria, Road Traffic Act 1988 s149, rescuers, contributory negligence, defences to nuisance and Rylands',
  summary: 'Even where all the elements of a tort are established, the defendant may have a defence. Volenti non fit injuria (consent) is a complete defence but is difficult to establish. Contributory negligence is a partial defence that reduces damages according to the claimant’s share of responsibility. Specific defences also apply to nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher.',
  spec: ['Volenti non fit injuria: must be voluntary; knowledge is not consent; effect of the Road Traffic Act 1988; position of rescuers', 'Contributory negligence: nature and effect; the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945', 'Defences specific to nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Volenti non fit injuria', html: `
<p>“To a willing person, no injury is done.” If the claimant freely and voluntarily agreed to run the risk of harm with full knowledge of its nature and extent, the defendant has a <b>complete defence</b>.</p>
<ul><li><b>Knowledge is not consent</b> — [[c:smithbaker]]. The claimant must have agreed to accept the risk of <b>injury</b>, not just known about it.</li>
<li><b>Must be voluntary</b> — employees are rarely volenti because economic pressure means they have no real choice; but see [[c:shatwell]], where the brothers deliberately broke safety rules.</li>
<li><b>Extreme and obvious risk</b> — [[c:morrismurray]] (flying with a very drunk pilot). Contrast [[c:dann]] (lift from a drinking driver — not volenti).</li>
<li><b>Road Traffic Act 1988 s149</b>: volenti cannot be used against a <b>passenger</b> in a motor vehicle where insurance is compulsory — [[c:pittshunt]]. The same principle stopped the defence in [[c:nettleship]].</li>
<li><b>Rescuers</b> are not volenti, because they act under a moral compulsion in an emergency — [[c:haynes]]; [[c:bakerhopkins]]. Rescuers include professionals ([[c:ogwo]]).</li>
<li>OLA 1957 s2(5) and OLA 1984 s1(6) apply volenti to occupiers. Sporting participants consent to ordinary risks of the game, not deliberate or reckless foul play.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Contributory negligence', html: `
<div class="box stat"><b class="lbl">Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 s1(1)</b><p>Where a person suffers damage partly as a result of their own fault and partly the fault of another, the claim is not defeated, but damages are reduced “to such extent as the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the claimant’s share in the responsibility for the damage”.</p></div>
<ul><li>The defendant must prove the claimant failed to take reasonable care for their <b>own safety</b>, and that this contributed to the <b>damage</b> (not necessarily the accident) — [[c:froom]]; [[c:jonesliviox]].</li>
<li>Guidelines: seatbelts — [[c:froom]] (25% / 15% / 0%); escaping in a dangerous way — [[c:sayers]] (25%); accepting a lift from a drunk driver — [[c:owens]] (20%); [[c:revill]] (burglar, two-thirds).</li>
<li><b>Children</b> are judged by the standard of a reasonable child of their age — [[c:gough]].</li>
<li>A 100% reduction is not possible — [[c:pittshunt]] (doubting [[c:jayes]]).</li>
<li>Emergencies: claimants are not negligent for reasonable reactions in “the agony of the moment”.</li></ul>
<p>Try the calculator in the <b>Explore</b> tab.</p>` },
    { h: 'Other defences', html: `
<ul><li><b>Illegality</b> (ex turpi causa) — a claimant cannot recover for harm suffered while jointly committing a crime — [[c:pittshunt]].</li>
<li><b>Nuisance</b> — prescription (20 years), statutory authority ([[c:allengulf]]); not coming to the nuisance or planning permission (topic 2.2.4b).</li>
<li><b>Rylands v Fletcher</b> — consent/common benefit ([[c:peters]]), act of a stranger ([[c:perry]]), Act of God ([[c:nichols]]), statutory authority ([[c:greenchelsea]]), default of the claimant ([[c:pontingnoakes]]) (topic 2.2.4c).</li>
<li><b>Trespass</b> — licence, lawful authority, necessity.</li>
<li><b>Limitation</b>: most tort claims must be brought within 6 years, personal injury within 3 years of the injury or date of knowledge (Limitation Act 1980).</li></ul>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Is contributory negligence a fairer defence than volenti?', for: ['Apportions loss according to responsibility — flexible and just.', 'Claimants still receive some compensation.', 'Volenti’s all-or-nothing effect is harsh, which is why courts rarely apply it.'], ag: ['Apportionment can be arbitrary (fixed percentages like Froom).', 'Volenti rightly bars claimants who deliberately accept extreme risks (Morris v Murray).', 'Contributory negligence may undercompensate vulnerable claimants.'] }],
  cases: ['smithbaker', 'shatwell', 'morrismurray', 'dann', 'pittshunt', 'nettleship', 'haynes', 'bakerhopkins', 'ogwo', 'froom', 'sayers', 'owens', 'jayes', 'jonesliviox', 'gough', 'revill', 'allengulf', 'peters', 'perry', 'nichols', 'greenchelsea', 'pontingnoakes'],
  worked: [{ q: '<b>Scenario.</b> Omar, 19, goes to a party with his friend Pia, who drinks heavily. Knowing she is drunk, he accepts a lift home and does not wear a seatbelt. Pia crashes. Omar suffers head injuries that a seatbelt would have made less severe. A passer-by, Raj, is hurt pulling Omar from the burning car. Advise Pia on any defences.', s: ['<b class="st">Volenti v Omar</b>Section 149 Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents Pia relying on volenti against a passenger. (Even at common law, Dann v Hamilton suggests he did not consent to injury.)', '<b class="st">Contributory negligence</b>Omar failed to take reasonable care for his own safety twice: accepting a lift from a driver he knew was drunk (Owens v Brimmell — about 20%) and not wearing a seatbelt that would have lessened his injuries (Froom v Butcher — 15%). The court may combine these (e.g. around 30–35%).', '<b class="st">Raj</b>A rescuer is not volenti (Haynes v Harwood; Baker v TE Hopkins), and the rescue does not break the chain of causation. Contributory negligence only if he acted wholly unreasonably.', '<b class="st">Conclude</b>Pia has no complete defence; Omar’s damages will be reduced; Raj recovers in full.'], a: 'No volenti; Omar’s damages reduced for contributory negligence; Raj recovers in full.' }],
  pitfalls: ['Treating knowledge of a risk as consent (Smith v Baker).', 'Applying volenti to motor passengers (s149 RTA 1988).', 'Saying rescuers are volenti.', 'Saying contributory negligence defeats the claim — it only reduces damages; 100% reductions are not possible.'],
  cards: [
    ['Volenti non fit injuria?', '“To a willing person no injury is done” — full, free consent to the risk; complete defence.'],
    ['Smith v Baker (1891)?', 'Knowledge of a risk is not consent to it.'],
    ['ICI v Shatwell (1965)?', 'Volenti applied — brothers deliberately broke safety rules.'],
    ['Morris v Murray (1991)?', 'Volenti — flying with an obviously drunk pilot.'],
    ['Road Traffic Act 1988 s149?', 'Volenti cannot be raised against passengers in motor vehicles.'],
    ['Are rescuers volenti?', 'No — Haynes v Harwood; Baker v TE Hopkins.'],
    ['LR(CN)A 1945 s1(1)?', 'Damages reduced as just and equitable according to the claimant’s share of responsibility.'],
    ['Froom v Butcher guidelines?', '25% if seatbelt would have prevented injury; 15% if reduced it; 0% if no difference.'],
    ['Sayers v Harlow UDC (1957)?', '25% reduction for climbing on a toilet-roll holder.'],
    ['Can damages be reduced by 100%?', 'No — Pitts v Hunt (1991) said it is logically impossible (Jayes v IMI, 1985, which allowed 100%, is now doubted).'],
    ['Gough v Thorne (1966)?', 'A child judged as a reasonable child of the same age — no contributory negligence.'],
    ['Pitts v Hunt (1991)?', 'Illegality defeated a pillion passenger’s claim.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Volenti non fit injuria is…', o: ['a complete defence', 'a partial defence', 'only for occupiers', 'a remedy'], x: 'Contributory negligence is partial.' },
    { q: 'Smith v Baker established that…', o: ['knowledge of a risk is not consent to it', 'employees always consent', 'volenti applies to rescuers', 'volenti needs writing'], x: 'Quarry crane.' },
    { q: 'Section 149 of the Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents…', o: ['volenti being used against passengers in motor vehicles', 'contributory negligence claims', 'rescuers from suing', 'drivers from being insured'], x: 'Pitts v Hunt.' },
    { q: 'Under Froom v Butcher, if a seatbelt would have prevented the injury entirely, damages are reduced by…', o: ['25%', '15%', '50%', '100%'], x: '15% if it would have reduced severity.' },
    { q: 'Rescuers are generally…', o: ['not treated as volenti', 'always volenti', 'contributorily negligent', 'trespassers'], x: 'Haynes v Harwood.' },
    { q: 'Which case held that a 100% reduction for contributory negligence is not possible?', o: ['Pitts v Hunt', 'Froom v Butcher', 'Owens v Brimmell', 'Sayers v Harlow'], x: 'The Act assumes shared responsibility. Jayes v IMI (1985) had allowed 100%.' },
    { q: 'In Morris v Murray the defence of volenti succeeded because…', o: ['the risk from the drunk pilot was so obvious and extreme', 'the claimant was an employee', 'there was a written waiver', 'the plane was a motor vehicle'], x: 'Planes are not covered by s149.' },
    { q: 'In Gough v Thorne a 13½-year-old was not contributorily negligent because…', o: ['she acted as a reasonable child of her age', 'children can never be negligent', 'the lorry driver consented', 'there was no crash'], x: 'Child standard.' }
  ],
  exam: [{ q: 'Explain the defences of volenti non fit injuria and contributory negligence. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['volenti — meaning; complete defence', 'knowledge not consent — Smith v Baker', 'voluntary — employees; Shatwell', 'Morris v Murray v Dann v Hamilton', 's149 RTA 1988; rescuers — Haynes', 'contributory negligence — 1945 Act s1(1)', 'Froom; Sayers; Owens', 'children — Gough; no 100% — Pitts v Hunt (doubting Jayes)'] }],
  tools: ['apportion', 'defencesort']
});

/* ---------- 2.2.7 Remedies ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.2.7', unit: 'TO', ref: '2.2.7', title: 'Remedies in tort',
  short: 'Compensatory damages (special and general), personal injury damages, mitigation of loss, injunctions',
  summary: 'The main remedy in tort is compensatory damages, which aim to put the claimant back in the position they would have been in had the tort not been committed. Personal injury damages cover both financial losses and pain, suffering and loss of amenity. The claimant must mitigate their loss. Injunctions stop continuing torts such as nuisance and trespass.',
  spec: ['Compensatory damages: the aim; special and general damages; pecuniary and non-pecuniary loss', 'Damages for personal injury: lump sums, structured settlements, provisional and interim damages', 'Other types: nominal, contemptuous, aggravated, exemplary damages', 'Damages on death', 'Mitigation of loss', 'Injunctions: prohibitory, mandatory, interim; damages in lieu'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Compensatory damages', html: `
<p>Tort damages aim to put the claimant, so far as money can, into the position they would have been in <b>if the tort had not been committed</b> (<i>Livingstone v Rawyards Coal</i>, 1880) — compare contract, which looks forward to performance.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Special damages</th><th>General damages</th></tr>
<tr><td>Specific, calculable financial losses up to trial: lost earnings, medical expenses, repair costs, travel</td><td>Losses not precisely calculable: <b>pain, suffering and loss of amenity</b> (PSLA), future loss of earnings, future care costs</td></tr></table></div>
<ul><li><b>Pain, suffering and loss of amenity</b> are valued using the <b>Judicial College Guidelines</b> and previous awards.</li>
<li><b>Future losses</b> are calculated using a <b>multiplicand</b> (annual loss) × <b>multiplier</b> (from the actuarial Ogden Tables), adjusted by the <b>personal injury discount rate</b> (set at +0.5% in England and Wales from January 2025).</li>
<li><b>Property damage</b>: cost of repair or diminution in value, plus consequential losses (e.g. hire of a replacement).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'How damages are paid', html: `
<ul><li><b>Lump sum</b> — the traditional method; a single final payment. Risk that it runs out or turns out too generous if the claimant recovers or dies early.</li>
<li><b>Periodical payments / structured settlements</b> — regular payments for life; courts can order them for future losses (Damages Act 1996 s2, as amended by the Courts Act 2003). Common in catastrophic injury claims.</li>
<li><b>Provisional damages</b> — where there is a chance of serious deterioration later (e.g. epilepsy), the claimant can return to court (Senior Courts Act 1981 s32A).</li>
<li><b>Interim payments</b> — part payments before trial where liability is admitted or likely.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Other types of damages', html: `
<ul><li><b>Nominal damages</b> — a small sum where a right was infringed but no loss suffered (e.g. trespass).</li>
<li><b>Contemptuous damages</b> — the smallest coin, where the claim succeeds but the court disapproves of the claimant bringing it.</li>
<li><b>Aggravated damages</b> — extra compensation for injury to feelings caused by the manner of the tort (e.g. humiliating treatment).</li>
<li><b>Exemplary (punitive) damages</b> — to punish, in the limited categories in [[c:rookes]].</li>
<li><b>Death</b>: the estate can continue claims (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934); dependants can claim for lost support and a fixed <b>bereavement award</b> under the <b>Fatal Accidents Act 1976</b>.</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Mitigation of loss', html: `
<p>The claimant must take <b>reasonable steps</b> to reduce their loss — e.g. accepting reasonable medical treatment, looking for suitable alternative work, hiring a reasonably priced replacement car. They cannot recover loss they could reasonably have avoided. The burden of proof is on the defendant, and the standard is not a demanding one — the claimant need not take risky or unreasonable steps.</p>` },
    { h: 'Injunctions', html: `
<ul><li><b>Prohibitory</b> — orders the defendant to stop (e.g. stop the noise, stop trespassing).</li>
<li><b>Mandatory</b> — orders the defendant to do something (e.g. remove an encroaching building).</li>
<li><b>Interim</b> — temporary, before trial: [[c:cyanamid]] (serious question to be tried; adequacy of damages; balance of convenience).</li>
<li><b>Quia timet</b> — to prevent a threatened wrong before it occurs.</li></ul>
<p>Injunctions are <b>discretionary</b>. The court may award <b>damages in lieu</b> (Senior Courts Act 1981 s50) — [[c:shelfer]]; [[c:coventry]]; [[c:millerjackson]]; [[c:dennis]]; contrast [[c:kennaway]].</p>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Is the lump sum system of damages satisfactory?', for: ['Finality — the claimant and defendant can move on.', 'Claimants control their money.', 'Simpler and cheaper to administer.'], ag: ['Guesswork about the future — life expectancy, health, inflation.', 'Money may run out, leaving the claimant dependent on the state.', 'Periodical payments better match needs (Damages Act 1996).', 'Discount rate changes can dramatically alter awards.'] }],
  cases: ['rookes', 'cyanamid', 'shelfer', 'coventry', 'millerjackson', 'dennis', 'kennaway'],
  worked: [],
  pitfalls: ['Confusing the aim of tort damages (restore pre-tort position) with contract damages (as if performed).', 'Mixing up special damages (quantified to trial) with general damages.', 'Saying exemplary damages are common.', 'Forgetting that injunctions are discretionary.'],
  cards: [
    ['Aim of tort damages?', 'To put the claimant in the position as if the tort had not occurred (Livingstone v Rawyards Coal).'],
    ['Special damages?', 'Calculable financial losses up to trial.'],
    ['General damages?', 'Non-calculable losses — PSLA, future loss of earnings and care.'],
    ['How are future losses calculated?', 'Multiplicand × multiplier (Ogden Tables), adjusted by the discount rate.'],
    ['Provisional damages?', 'Allow the claimant to return to court if a specified serious deterioration occurs.'],
    ['Periodical payments?', 'Regular payments for future loss, ordered under the Damages Act 1996.'],
    ['Nominal v contemptuous damages?', 'Nominal: right infringed, no loss. Contemptuous: tiny sum showing disapproval of the claim.'],
    ['Exemplary damages categories?', 'Oppressive government action; conduct calculated to profit; statute (Rookes v Barnard).'],
    ['Fatal Accidents Act 1976?', 'Dependants’ claims and bereavement award.'],
    ['American Cyanamid test?', 'Serious question to be tried; adequacy of damages; balance of convenience.'],
    ['Mitigation of loss?', 'Claimant must take reasonable steps to reduce loss.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Tort damages aim to put the claimant in the position they would have been in…', o: ['if the tort had not happened', 'if a contract had been performed', 'if they had been more careful', 'at the date of trial'], x: 'Restitutio in integrum.' },
    { q: 'Pain, suffering and loss of amenity are part of…', o: ['general damages', 'special damages', 'nominal damages', 'exemplary damages'], x: 'Judicial College Guidelines.' },
    { q: 'Lost earnings from the accident to the trial date are…', o: ['special damages', 'general damages', 'contemptuous damages', 'aggravated damages'], x: 'Calculable.' },
    { q: 'Which remedy allows a claimant to return to court if their condition seriously deteriorates?', o: ['Provisional damages', 'Interim payment', 'Nominal damages', 'Injunction'], x: 'Senior Courts Act 1981 s32A.' },
    { q: 'The test for interim injunctions comes from…', o: ['American Cyanamid v Ethicon', 'Shelfer', 'Rookes v Barnard', 'Hadley v Baxendale'], x: 'Balance of convenience.' },
    { q: 'An injunction ordering a defendant to remove a wall is…', o: ['mandatory', 'prohibitory', 'interim', 'quia timet'], x: 'Requires positive action.' },
    { q: 'Exemplary damages are…', o: ['punitive and limited to specific categories', 'always awarded in personal injury', 'the same as aggravated damages', 'nominal'], x: 'Rookes v Barnard.' }
  ],
  exam: [{ q: 'Explain the remedies available in tort. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['aim of compensatory damages', 'special v general damages', 'PSLA; future loss — multiplier', 'lump sums v periodical payments; provisional; interim', 'nominal, contemptuous, aggravated, exemplary (Rookes)', 'Fatal Accidents Act', 'mitigation', 'injunctions — types; Cyanamid; damages in lieu (Shelfer/Coventry)'] }],
  tools: ['tortremedysort']
});

